# pi-superagent settings.
#
# Copy this file to config.env in the state directory (~/.pi/superagent unless PI_SUPERAGENT_STATE says
# otherwise), edit it, and run `superagent restart`. The start script reads it on every start, so
# a restart by hand and a restart by the health check run the same router. A variable already set in the
# environment wins over this file.
#
# One KEY=value per line, taken literally: no quotes, no $VARIABLES, no ~, and no comment after a value.
# Each commented-out line shows a setting's default.

# ---- GitHub, the only way in ----

# The repository to watch. Unset, the router runs with intake switched off.
PI_SUPERAGENT_GITHUB_REPO=your-org/your-repo

# The account the agent replies from, if it has one of its own: its comments are never requests. Leave it
# unset when you and the agent share an account. The agent's replies then carry a hidden marker instead,
# and naming your own account here would discard every comment you write.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_GITHUB_SELF=your-agent-bot

# The agent's own GitHub account: a file holding a token for it (classic, repo scope), read on every call.
# The router then reads the watched repository and posts every reply as that account. Give the account
# access to the watched repository, set PI_SUPERAGENT_GITHUB_SELF to its login, and keep the file readable
# only by you (chmod 600). Workers still act as the account gh is signed in as, and the router reads their
# pull requests as that account too, since it opened them. Unset, the router acts as gh's account.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_GITHUB_TOKEN_FILE=/absolute/path/to/token

# Author patterns that are bots, never people. A * matches anything.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_BOTS=pi-*,fleet-*

# Who the router hears. Unset, only people with write access to the repository: GitHub's OWNER, MEMBER
# (any member of the owning organization) and COLLABORATOR. Everyone else's issues, comments and reviews
# are dropped before the judge sees them, because a request starts a worker with a shell on this machine.
# Set, this comma-separated list of logins replaces that rule, so include your own. A * matches anything.
# The watched repository is where the operator asks, not the code the worker changes: set it and a worker gets
# no checkout of the ask board, and is told to clone the repository the request names. Unset suits a
# single-repository deployment, where the watched repository is the code.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_WATCH_REPO_ASKS=1

#PI_SUPERAGENT_ALLOWED=your-login,a-teammate

# ---- the router ----

# Milliseconds between polls of GitHub when nothing else wakes the router.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_TICK_MS=30000

# Liveness of that loop, measured live 2026-11-09: a 30 s timer entered 7 ticks in 6.6 h and nothing said so.
# A pass that has not finished in TICK_STALL_MS names its stage in engine.log and in health.json, where
# `superagent status` fails on it; a pass past TICK_ABANDON_MS is given up on and a fresh one started.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_TICK_STALL_MS=60000
#PI_SUPERAGENT_TICK_STALL_REPORT_MS=60000
#PI_SUPERAGENT_TICK_ABANDON_MS=300000

# How long a pass may start the launches it queued. It never waits for a free worker slot: that queue is the
# one that filled the stall above. Raise it only if launches are slow to begin and the beat can spare the time.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_LAUNCH_DRAIN_MS=5000

# Followed pull requests one pass reads, and the milliseconds it spends starting them. A pass reads each one at
# most every 120 s; these bound the pass that finds them all due at once (41 follows measured 12 s).
#PI_SUPERAGENT_PULL_READS_PER_TICK=16
#PI_SUPERAGENT_PULL_READ_BUDGET_MS=10000

# Minutes after which a health.json nobody rewrote is itself the failure: the loop that publishes that file is
# the loop that died, and a pass blocked synchronously cannot report its own stall. 30 beats at the default tick.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_HEALTH_STALE_MIN=15

# The panel's port, on 127.0.0.1.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_PANEL_PORT=8788

# The judge's thinking level, in pi's names for them.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_JUDGE_THINKING=low

# Seconds `superagent stop` waits for the router to exit before closing its tab.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_GRACE=30

# Seconds `superagent start` waits for the router to report ready; past it, start exits 1 so a scheduled
# health check restarts a router that exists in herdr but never came up.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_READY_WAIT=60

# ---- herdr ----

# The herdr binary, if it is not on PATH.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_HERDR=herdr

# The workspace that holds the router and its workers.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_HERDR_WORKSPACE=superagent

# The router's agent name in herdr.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_SESSION=superagent

# ---- workers ----

# Workers on a run at once. Workers parked between runs do not count; further tickets wait their turn.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_MAX_WORKERS=4

# An absolute path for the per-ticket workspaces. The default is the work directory in the state directory.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_WORKTREES=/absolute/path/to/workspaces

# By default a new worker clones the watched repository and checks out superagent/<ticket-id>.
# Set this command to replace the default checkout. It runs with PI_SUPERAGENT_TICKET and
# PI_SUPERAGENT_DIR set. It runs again whenever the ticket gets a fresh worker, so make it safe to repeat.
# If it fails, the ticket fails with the command's own output.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_PREPARE=[ -d .git ] || git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/your-org/your-repo .

# Command names a worker may not run, comma-separated. Names only: no arguments, no paths.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_BLOCKED=gh,ssh

# Set to 1 to let workers start subagents, workflows and other sessions. Delegated work escapes the
# ticket's mandate, workspace and report, so it is off by default.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_ALLOW_DELEGATION=1

# Settled ticket checkouts to keep at router start, newest first (default 20). Each one is a full clone,
# measured about 570 MB, and nothing deleted them before: 75 tickets filled 43 GB.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_KEEP_WORKTREES=20

# A file holding a fine-grained GitHub token (one repository, contents: read+write) that a worker uses to push
# its branch and open its pull request. The worker's HOME is its own, so it has no login of the operator's:
# unset, the worker commits in its checkout and reports the branch instead of being told to push.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_WORKER_TOKEN_FILE=/path/to/worker-token

# Report a worker that has said nothing for this many minutes (default 15). It reports; it does not stop it.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_WORKER_QUIET_MINUTES=15

# Whose requests the router hears. Unset, it accepts any author GitHub rates as OWNER, MEMBER or
# COLLABORATOR, and MEMBER means any member of the organization that owns the repository. Set the list to
# close that to your own login.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_ALLOWED=your-login

# Times one ticket may report the same result with nothing new on its thread before it stops recruiting
# sessions and says so on the issue (default 3). The count restarts when a person writes on the thread, or
# when the report changes state; the router's own receipt and the intake label do not restart it. Measured
# 2026-11-09: one ticket reported 93 times in 20 hours, another five times in nine, each report a whole pi
# session with the full gate set run again on a commit that had merged two days earlier.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_REPORT_CAP=3

# Set to 1 to leave the watched repository's issue OPEN when its ticket finishes with nothing to wait on. Off
# by default, because an open issue is a request intake hears again: the ticket ends, the board re-asks, a
# worker re-proves it is done. Commenting on a closed issue, or reopening it, still starts the work again.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_KEEP_ISSUES_OPEN=1

# Checkouts this deployment reads besides its own and the ticket clones, comma- or colon-separated. Each one
# is scanned for a credential baked into a remote URL: git writes .git/config 0644, so a token in a URL is
# readable by every worker session on the box, and the first `git remote -v` prints it into a transcript.
# A hit fails the deploy and reports status 3. See docs/operator-guide.md, "Keep the git credential out of
# .git/config", for the 0600-file-and-helper shape that replaces it.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_WATCHED_CHECKOUTS=/home/you/git/pi-superagent:/home/you/git/other-checkout
